    "agent_last_message": "The tool is built and working. `cmake -B build && cmake --build build` compiles it with no warnings and produces `build/wordcount`. All 17 of my test cases matched a separate Python script I wrote to check the results.\n\n**Files in `output/`:**\n- `CMakeLists.txt`: C++20, standard library only, defaults to a Release build.\n- `src/main.cpp`: reads `--input` and `--output`, sets the exit codes, and catches unexpected errors.\n- `src/analyzer.{hpp,cpp}`: reads the file in 1 MiB chunks and counts lines, words and characters, so large files don't need to fit in memory.\n- `src/word_table.hpp`: a fast lookup table for word counts.\n- `src/json_writer.{hpp,cpp}`: writes the JSON result.\n\n**Testing**\n- I checked the spec's edge cases: empty file, whitespace only, with and without a trailing newline, `\\n\\n\\n`, mixed case, punctuation, and ties sorted alphabetically.\n- I also checked random files of 4\u20135 MB and words that straddle the 1 MiB chunk boundary. That test caught a real bug: two words could be merged across the boundary. It's fixed and covered by tests now.\n- **Exit codes:** these all exit 1:\n  - no arguments, or `--input` or `--output` missing\n  - a flag with no value, an unknown flag, a repeated flag, or an extra word on the command line\n  - an input file that doesn't exist, can't be read, or is a directory\n  - an output location that can't be written to\n\n  Exit 2 is for internal errors such as running out of memory.\n- **Speed:** about 190 MB/s on realistic text (237 MB in 1.3 s) and 2.3 s for a 223 MB worst-case file.\n\n**Choices the spec doesn't settle:**\n- **Lowercasing is ASCII only** (A\u2013Z). The spec counts bytes, so I kept everything byte-based. As a result, `CAF\u00c9` lowercases to `caf\u00c9`, not `caf\u00e9`. Full Unicode lowercasing without external libraries would mean shipping large lookup tables.\n- **No input is rejected as \"malformed\".** The spec says every byte counts as a character, so files with non-UTF-8 bytes are still analyzed. Those bytes appear in the JSON as `\\u0",
